{
 "id": "otp-service",
 "title": "OTP Service",
 "pattern": "hashing",
 "level": "medium",
 "statement": "<p>You are building the core of a one-time password (OTP) service, like the one behind an SMS login. It is a pure state machine: the clock and the codes are given to you, so tests are repeatable.</p><p>Write <code>def solution(events)</code>. Each event is <code>[kind, phone, t, code]</code>: <code>kind</code> is <code>\"request\"</code> or <code>\"verify\"</code>, <code>phone</code> and <code>code</code> are strings, <code>t</code> is an integer time in seconds. Times never decrease. Return a list with one result string per event, in order.</p><p><b>request</b> (send <code>code</code> to <code>phone</code>):</p><ul><li>If this phone had an <i>accepted</i> request less than 30 seconds ago (<code>t - last &lt; 30</code>), return <code>\"RATE_LIMITED\"</code> and change nothing.</li><li>Otherwise return <code>\"SENT\"</code>. The new OTP replaces any old one for this phone, with a fresh expiry and a fresh count of 0 wrong attempts.</li></ul><p><b>verify</b> (the user types <code>code</code>). Check these rules in this order and return the first that applies:</p><ol><li><code>\"NO_OTP\"</code>: the phone has no OTP, either because none was ever sent or because the last one was already used successfully.</li><li><code>\"LOCKED\"</code>: the OTP already had 3 wrong attempts.</li><li><code>\"EXPIRED\"</code>: <code>t - issued &ge; 300</code> (an OTP is valid while <code>t - issued &lt; 300</code>). This does not count as an attempt.</li><li><code>\"OK\"</code>: the code matches. The OTP is used up.</li><li><code>\"WRONG\"</code>: the code does not match. Count the attempt; the 3rd wrong attempt still returns <code>\"WRONG\"</code> but locks the OTP.</li></ol><p>Phones never affect each other. Codes are compared as exact strings (<code>\"012345\"</code> is not <code>\"12345\"</code>).</p>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "events = [[\"request\", \"90000001\", 0, \"482915\"], [\"verify\", \"90000001\", 10, \"000000\"], [\"verify\", \"90000001\", 20, \"482915\"], [\"verify\", \"90000001\", 25, \"482915\"]]",
   "output": "[\"SENT\", \"WRONG\", \"OK\", \"NO_OTP\"]",
   "why": "One wrong guess, then the right code. A successful verify uses the OTP up, so typing it again finds nothing."
  },
  {
   "input": "events = [[\"request\", \"90000002\", 0, \"111111\"], [\"verify\", \"90000002\", 5, \"222222\"], [\"verify\", \"90000002\", 6, \"333333\"], [\"verify\", \"90000002\", 7, \"444444\"], [\"verify\", \"90000002\", 8, \"111111\"], [\"request\", \"90000002\", 20, \"555555\"], [\"request\", \"90000002\", 30, \"666666\"], [\"verify\", \"90000002\", 31, \"666666\"]]",
   "output": "[\"SENT\", \"WRONG\", \"WRONG\", \"WRONG\", \"LOCKED\", \"RATE_LIMITED\", \"SENT\", \"OK\"]",
   "why": "Three wrong guesses lock the OTP, so even the right code is refused. A new request at t=20 is only 20 s after the accepted one at 0, so it is rate limited. At t=30 (exactly 30 s later) it is accepted and resets everything."
  },
  {
   "input": "events = [[\"request\", \"90000003\", 0, \"135790\"], [\"verify\", \"90000003\", 299, \"999999\"], [\"verify\", \"90000003\", 300, \"135790\"], [\"verify\", \"90000004\", 300, \"135790\"]]",
   "output": "[\"SENT\", \"WRONG\", \"EXPIRED\", \"NO_OTP\"]",
   "why": "At t=299 the OTP is still live (299 &lt; 300), so the guess is just wrong. At t=300 it has expired, even with the right code. Phone ...04 never asked for an OTP."
  }
