 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What state does the service keep per phone?",
   "a": "The time of the last <i>accepted</i> request (for the rate limit), and the current OTP if any: its code, when it was issued, and how many wrong attempts it has had. Nothing else matters."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "Request at 0 with <code>\"111111\"</code>, then three wrong verifies, then the right one.",
   "a": "SENT; WRONG (1); WRONG (2); WRONG (3, now locked); the right code returns LOCKED because the lock rule is checked before the code. That is the point of a lock: after 3 misses, guessing the 4th time must not work."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What is the first thing you would write?",
   "a": "For every event, scan back through all earlier events for the same phone to work out the last accepted request, the attempts since, and whether it was used. It works, but it is <code>O(n&sup2;)</code> and the rules end up spread across several loops."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Clue words?",
   "a": "\"per phone\", \"replaces\", \"attempts\" &rarr; <b>hash map of state</b>. Map <code>phone &rarr; OTP record</code> and <code>phone &rarr; last accepted request time</code>. Each event is <code>O(1)</code>. Then encode the rule order literally as a chain of early returns, so the precedence in the spec is visible in the code."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li><code>otps = {}</code> (phone &rarr; <code>[code, issued, wrong]</code>), <code>last_sent = {}</code>.</li><li>request: if <code>phone in last_sent</code> and <code>t - last_sent[phone] &lt; 30</code>, RATE_LIMITED. Else store a fresh record, set <code>last_sent[phone] = t</code>, SENT.</li><li>verify: no record &rarr; NO_OTP; <code>wrong &ge; 3</code> &rarr; LOCKED; <code>t - issued &ge; 300</code> &rarr; EXPIRED; match &rarr; delete the record, OK; else <code>wrong += 1</code>, WRONG.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "Verify at exactly 299 and 300 s. Request at exactly 29 and 30 s after the last accepted one. A rate-limited request must not reset the old OTP or the 30 s clock. A locked OTP that has also expired (LOCKED wins). A new request after a lock. Codes with leading zeros. Two phones interleaved."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "Keep two dicts keyed by phone: one for the current OTP, one for the time of the last <b>accepted</b> request. A rejected request touches neither.",
  "For verify, write the checks as early returns in exactly the order the statement lists them: NO_OTP, LOCKED, EXPIRED, then compare the code.",
  "Store the OTP as a small mutable record like <code>[code, issued, wrong]</code>. On OK, <code>del otps[phone]</code>; on WRONG, <code>rec[2] += 1</code>. A record with <code>rec[2] &gt;= 3</code> is locked."
 ],
 "solution": "RATE_LIMIT = 30      # seconds between accepted requests per phone\nTTL = 300            # an OTP is valid while t - issued < TTL\nMAX_WRONG = 3        # wrong attempts before the OTP locks\n\ndef solution(events):\n    otps = {}        # phone -> [code, issued, wrong_attempts]\n    last_sent = {}   # phone -> time of the last ACCEPTED request\n    out = []\n    for kind, phone, t, code in events:\n        if kind == 'request':\n            if phone in last_sent and t - last_sent[phone] < RATE_LIMIT:\n                out.append('RATE_LIMITED')          # old OTP and clock untouched\n                continue\n            otps[phone] = [code, t, 0]              # replaces any old OTP\n            last_sent[phone] = t\n            out.append('SENT')\n            continue\n        # verify: the rule order below IS the spec's precedence\n        rec = otps.get(phone)\n        if rec is None:\n            out.append('NO_OTP')\n        elif rec[2] >= MAX_WRONG:\n            out.append('LOCKED')\n        elif t - rec[1] >= TTL:\n            out.append('EXPIRED')\n        elif code == rec[0]:\n            del otps[phone]                         # one-time: use it up\n            out.append('OK')\n        else:\n            rec[2] += 1                             # the 3rd miss locks it\n            out.append('WRONG')\n    return out\n",
 "brute": "def solution(events):\n    # No stored state: rebuild everything from the earlier events and their results. O(n^2)\n    out = []\n    for i in range(len(events)):\n        kind, phone, t, code = events[i]\n        last = -1                                   # index of the latest accepted request\n        for j in range(i):\n            if events[j][1] == phone and events[j][0] == 'request' and out[j] == 'SENT':\n                last = j\n        if kind == 'request':\n            if last >= 0 and t - events[last][2] < 30:\n                out.append('RATE_LIMITED')\n            else:\n                out.append('SENT')\n            continue\n        if last < 0:\n            out.append('NO_OTP')\n            continue\n        wrong = 0\n        used = False\n        for j in range(last + 1, i):\n            if events[j][1] == phone and events[j][0] == 'verify':\n                if out[j] == 'OK':\n                    used = True\n                if out[j] == 'WRONG':\n                    wrong += 1\n        if used:\n            out.append('NO_OTP')\n        elif wrong >= 3:\n            out.append('LOCKED')\n        elif t - events[last][2] >= 300:\n            out.append('EXPIRED')\n        elif code == events[last][3]:\n            out.append('OK')\n        else:\n            out.append('WRONG')\n    return out\n",
 "explain": "<p>The whole service is two dictionaries and a fixed order of checks. Walk example 2:</p><ul><li>t=0 request: no <code>last_sent</code> entry &rarr; store <code>['111111', 0, 0]</code>, SENT.</li><li>t=5, 6, 7 wrong codes: not locked, not expired, no match &rarr; attempts 1, 2, 3, each WRONG.</li><li>t=8 right code: the record has 3 wrong attempts &rarr; LOCKED. The lock is checked before the code, otherwise a 4th guess could still win.</li><li>t=20 request: <code>20 - 0 &lt; 30</code> &rarr; RATE_LIMITED, nothing changes.</li><li>t=30 request: <code>30 - 0 = 30</code>, not &lt; 30 &rarr; fresh record <code>['666666', 30, 0]</code>, SENT.</li><li>t=31 right code &rarr; OK and the record is deleted.</li></ul><p>Why the limits exist: a 6-digit code has 1,000,000 values. With 3 attempts per OTP and one OTP per 30 s, an attacker gets about 6 guesses a minute, a chance of about 6 in 1,000,000 per minute, instead of trying every code in seconds. In a pairing round, say this, name the constants, and write a test for each rule as you add it.</p>",
 "complexity": "O(n) time (O(1) dict work per event), O(phones) extra space",
